 even having been described as  eating a whole Ox ate salmon and three  whole gallons of mead in a single  sitting many stories in Norse myth  revolve around on Thor just wanting to  go out and get drunk one story even  involves Thor going all the way out of  his way to find an excellent cauldron to  brew Alin For A Feast of the Gods  traveling all the way Far East and  causing trouble with other gods along  the way to secure the kettle so right  away God of War Ragnarok accurately  depicts Thor as it aligns with Norse  mythology yet here is where God of War's  depiction of the Thunder God begins to  differ from Norse myth while in Norse  mythology Thor is undoubtedly an  imperfect hero often being  short-tempered easily enraged and even  somewhat racist towards Giants and  dwarves he's generally an honorable  Warrior and ideal to be aspired to often  defending the honor of those around him  in one tale Thor arrived at a feast to  find the visiting giant herognir drunk  and boasting about how he could kill all  the other gods including Freya and Sif  Thor's wife Thor angry at hierognir for  dishonoring his family lifted his hammer  mjolnir in order to kill hiragi here  right there but the giant stopped him by  accusing Thor of cowardice for  attempting to murder an unarmed man and  challenged him instead to a duel Thor  accepted the challenge and the next day  eventually killed ragnir in the duel by  hitting his head with his hammer so hard  that his head split open but at the same  time the giant managed to permanently  launch a piece of his Whetstone in  Thor's head for his troubles so all in  all an honorable Duel at least by a Seer  standards yet in God of War 2018 this  story is revealed to have been a  fabrication a story told to make Thor  seem better than he actually was I saw  the AC Allah leapt upon his shield and  swore he'd kill us all and take out  women fought back to jotenheim  Dan Thor shows up and just heal off oh  no  Thor takes one look at the drunken Stone  buffoon and brings down mjolnir on his  head so hard that he's got chunks of  lunia in his own skull to this day  I thought it's so startled by the face  full of rock he doesn't notice sunnier's  body topple right onto him with a  sickening crunch  and again  the Roars of laughter echoed through the  palace Halls  that's an awful story my mirror nothing  like the one's mother told me  might that be a lesson my son  truth is seldom so pretty as myth  enlightened this version of the tale  told by mimir in the game reveals that  Thor just showed up and felt slighted by  hirogne or jokingly telling stories  about him and killed him out of his  disgraced honor all while the gods  laughed as they watched on as a man died  uncaring either way this less honorable  version of Thor stands in sharp contrast  to the Thor of myth to the Scandinavian  Vikings Thor was the god of the people  Gods like Odin were the elites worthy of  worship but consistently above others  wielding magic and other powers that an  average person could never touch in  comparison while certainly stronger than  an average human Thor's power came  simply from his strength something that  any human could have and was an  attribute that many in Viking Society  valued Thor was relatable a hero who you  could quite literally have a beer with  this is not so much true for the Thor in  God of War while we never actually meet  Thor in God of War 2018 we do see his  effects on the lives of people that he's  encountered in one side quest you  encounter the spirit of a man whose  family worshiped Thor  my father passed my mother built the  statute  of grave imagine our surprise when the  God of Thunder himself came to offer  condolences  at first we were thrilled that he  quickly took advantage of our  hospitality  my mother begged him to leave and Thor  in his drunken rage killed her  I was left with nothing but grief  eventually I lost that as well  found only rage in its place  here we can see that the Thor of God of  War takes what he wants when he wants it  regardless of the consequences to  anybody else even to those who worship  him as Kratos himself says of this story  why did the gods cause so much trouble  for us because that is their  name this is perhaps the most  significant departure between Norse myth  and God of War's depiction of Thor where  Thor in mythology was relatable compared  to Odin for example both Thor and Odin  in the game are the elites who care  nothing for the Mortal Lies Beneath them  yet this depiction actually fits in with  the repeated theme of the entire God of  War franchise from the first game in the  series it has repeatedly been shown that  the gods whether they be Norse or Greek  care not for the lives of mortal men  the gods of Olympus have abandoned me in  the original trilogy on the PlayStation  2 and Playstation 3 Kratos is used as a  pawn in the Greek gods machinations who  don't really care for him as a person  even going so far as to bind him to  their service by tricking him into  killing his family after learning of  this Kratos became fueled and blinded by  rage and Kratos eventually murders the  entire Greek pantheon in his quest for  vengeance by the time of God of War 2018  though Kratos has managed to temper this  rage through his family but he  recognizes the same uncaring attitude in  the Norse gods that he saw in the Greek  ones as well as the lies that these Gods  tell Mortals to uphold the Mythos around  them a lesson he tries to pass on to his  son atreus with all that power you would  think at least some of them would try to  make life better for people and yet the  gods continue to spread misery Mrs life  boy so this depiction of Thor in God of  War despite not being completely aligned  with how Scandinavians viewed Thor in  myth fits perfectly within God of War's  themes and uses Norse mythology as  evidence within the story to affirm this  depiction of the Gods lies  I always knew Odie was bad  that's just useless automatic heartless  that's Odin in fact we would do well to  sit here in silence for the next few  moments and reflect on Odin's capacity  for cruelty yet Thor in the games also  reflects another theme how those in  power like the gods will do anything to  hold on to that power and the backstory  to God of War 2018 Thor learns of the  upcoming events of the World's End  Ragnarok where he is fated to die in a  battle with the world serpent  jormungound paranoid Thor has the  dwarves Brock and syndry make his hammer  mjolnir so that he might use it in order  to stop the events from coming to pass  he also makes his children soldiers  focused only on preventing Ragnarok and  even attempts to steal the Giant's  ability to see the future from the yotin  the Giants who managed to stop his  attempts upset by this failure Thor  turns his anger towards the yotin  killing many in his path however this  bloodlust eventually leads to Thor's  downfall as he is eventually stopped by  jormungound itself causing the very  rivalry that would eventually lead to  their final fight at the end of the  world in Ragnarok by attempting to stop  Ragnarok Thor slowly began to cause it  much of this story rhymes with Norse  Legend but it is distinctly different  from it for one Thor's hammer mjolnir is  made by Brock and syndrian myth but more  due to a playful bet by Loki a story I  discussed in a separate video on the  mythology behind Loki in the series but  moving on the Thor of Legend also had a  similar rivalry with yormign gown who in  this version is Loki's child but it's  more a battle of equals not one of  grudging hate many gods even attempt to  stop Thor and Norman gown from facing  each other in order to avoid Ragnarok  such as when the god himir cuts a  fishing line in order to prevent Thor  from Catching the serpent however while  the gods indeed fear and wish to prevent  Ragnarok and myth it is something that  is often avoided rather than something  actively attempted to be stopped as we  see in God of War yet this is ultimately  the story that God of War as a Series  has always been about the uncaring drive  of those in power towards those they  control and their unending willingness  to hold on to that power at the expense  of all others even themselves it speaks  perhaps to the two distinct eras in  which these stories are told Vikings of  old prided individual achievement with  Fame and honor being considered the only  things that carried on after you died  earning yourself a place in the annals  of History by your glorious deeds and  heroism ideals that the Thor of myth  reflected today however much of our  cultural conversation centers upon  issues of power who deserves it and  should anyone even have it in the first  place we today are much more likely to  distrust someone who seeks power for its  own sake and seems uncaring for the  collective good so using Thor as a  critique as a symbol of this class  discussion modernizes the context of  this character and also makes him fit  within the ideas that God of War  Ragnarok and the franchise as a whole  has always attempted to tackle head-on  or heads off depending on what Kratos  does to you but with all that said this  is where we leave Thor for now but if  you want to learn more about Norse  mythology and how it may tie into God of  War Ragnarok don't forget to subscribe  to this channel as we'll have another  God of lore video next week and in the  meantime check out all of our coverage  on God of War Ragnarok right here on  Gamespot
 

